¶ditem©dbodyyDuring the 2026-07-20 levi-hub-01 incident, the hub kept writing ~8k rows/s to Postgres for ~30 minutes AFTER the flooding client died: millions of duplicated events sat in myko-server's in-memory queues. Candidates: flume::unbounded ProducerRequest queue (libs/myko/server/src/postgres.rs:301), unbounded saga event channel (libs/myko/server/src/lib.rs:272, one more per saga at :438), and per-connection unbounded mpsc channels (ws_handler.rs:279-280). A malfunctioning or malicious client can grow server memory without bound and lag the write path for everyone; nothing propagates backpressure to the socket. Fix direction: bound the ingest/persist queues so TCP backpressure reaches the client (client-side WouldBlock handling is now correct after lv-a2ae), being careful not to deadlock saga-emitted events that re-enter the queue. Consider ingest dedupe of identical content-addressed SETs as a cheap mitigation. Cross-project: levi filed lv-39ad (backpressure) and lv-5f11 (ingest dedupe) on their side ‚Äî the real fix is here in myko-server.gcreatedx2026-07-21T04:21:51.532814ZlcreatedByDevxtrevor.a.sargent@gmail.compcreatedByMachinegmalcolmbidx a544cd9e296d432f85e6d086a5d838cfflabelsÅfserverhprioritybP2iprojectIdx de9416570df4447d8687b0103b13ae27etitlex]server: unbounded ingest/persist channels buffer without limit ‚Äî no backpressure to clientsjchangeTypecSEThitemTypedTaskicreatedAtx2026-07-21T04:21:51.532821Zbtxx$4dc2fc9d-2d50-4e6b-8b80-5f9c1cb5a2b1hsourceIdgmalcolm